1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,666
MedlinePlus presents Antibiotics versus Bacteria: Fighting the Resistance

2
00:00:06,666 --> 00:00:08,766
What if we couldn’t fight back?

3
00:00:08,766 --> 00:00:12,466
Tuberculosis. Gonorrhea. MRSA.

4
00:00:12,466 --> 00:00:19,800
These bad bugs are considered by the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases, or NIAID,

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,466
to be some of the most threatening organisms on the planet today.

6
00:00:23,466 --> 00:00:25,700
They have all joined the RESISTANCE.

7
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:29,766
That’s antimicrobial resistance, to be clear.

8
00:00:29,766 --> 00:00:34,233
Bacteria like these are quickly gaining the ability to thwart our antibiotics,

9
00:00:34,233 --> 00:00:38,700
leaving infections much more difficult to treat. And that’s a big problem.

10
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:40,900
The CDC estimates that every year,

11
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:46,000
more than two million people in the US get sick from antimicrobial resistant infections,

12
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,166
resulting in at least 23,000 deaths.

13
00:00:49,166 --> 00:00:54,300
The worry is that other bacteria could join this resistance faster than we can develop solutions,

14
00:00:54,300 --> 00:00:58,266
or that bacteria become impervious to even more antibiotics,

15
00:00:58,266 --> 00:01:03,066
leading to essentially untreatable diseases.

16
00:01:03,066 --> 00:01:04,933
Who are these bacteria?

17
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:09,333
Many different varieties of bacteria have developed antimicrobial resistance,

18
00:01:09,333 --> 00:01:11,766
but some are more worrying than others.

19
00:01:11,766 --> 00:01:16,700
Tuberculosis, or TB, is the number one infectious disease killer in the world,

20
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:20,166
taking over one and a half million lives every year.

21
00:01:20,166 --> 00:01:22,100
TB is difficult to treat,

22
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:26,133
and some resistant strains need years of daily treatment with multiple drugs,

23
00:01:26,133 --> 00:01:31,966
including months of painful injections and serious side effects that can leave patients deaf.

24
00:01:31,966 --> 00:01:37,800
Gonorrhea is worrisome because strains have become resistant to all but a few antibiotics.

25
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:42,566
This sexually transmitted disease can share its resistance genes between bacteria,

26
00:01:42,566 --> 00:01:46,066
increasing the speed of resistance.

27
00:01:46,066 --> 00:01:49,933
Staphylococcus aureus, or Staph, is everywhere:

28
00:01:49,933 --> 00:01:54,700
on our personal items, our skin, in our noses.

29
00:01:54,700 --> 00:01:56,700
Staph is usually not harmful.

30
00:01:56,700 --> 00:02:03,033
But when it is, it can be difficult to treat especially in cases of Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus,

31
00:02:03,033 --> 00:02:08,000
or MRSA, which is now carried by 2% of Americans.

32
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,233
These are just some of the leading bacteria in the resistance.

33
00:02:11,233 --> 00:02:13,733
There are others, and more are coming.

34
00:02:13,733 --> 00:02:15,900
How does resistance occur?

35
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:22,600
Resistance is happening quickly because of overuse and misuse of antibiotics over a long period of time,

36
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:26,266
such as not completing antibiotic courses as prescribed,

37
00:02:26,266 --> 00:02:31,366
and using antibiotics in agriculture to promote growth in animals.

38
00:02:31,366 --> 00:02:36,000
Bacteria multiply so fast, that even if we had the perfect antibiotic,

39
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,133
resistance would still occur.

40
00:02:39,133 --> 00:02:42,633
And every time we use antibiotics,

41
00:02:42,633 --> 00:02:46,900
there is a chance that some bacteria survive due to changes in their DNA.

42
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:50,866
The DNA can code for survival advantages such as:

43
00:02:50,866 --> 00:02:56,000
Changing the bacterial cell surface, preventing antibiotics from attaching or getting in.

44
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,466
Making pumps that spit the antibiotics out before they have a chance to work.

45
00:03:00,466 --> 00:03:04,700
Or creating enzymes that “neutralize” the antibiotics.

46
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:10,033
Antibiotics will kill most bacteria, including the helpful bacteria in our body.

47
00:03:10,033 --> 00:03:14,233
But the bacteria with the advantages can survive and reproduce.

48
00:03:14,233 --> 00:03:18,733
The resistant bacteria can pass the DNA changes on to their offspring,

49
00:03:18,733 --> 00:03:20,666
or sometimes even to each other,

50
00:03:20,666 --> 00:03:25,600
to generate new strains of antibiotic resistant bacteria.

51
00:03:25,600 --> 00:03:28,100
What can you do to fight the resistance?

52
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:33,100
Using fewer antibiotics as a society can help prevent resistance,

53
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:36,433
saving antibiotics for when they are most appropriate.

54
00:03:36,433 --> 00:03:40,966
The first step is preventing the need for antibiotics by avoiding infections,

55
00:03:40,966 --> 00:03:47,266
for example through hand-washing, immunizations, and safe food preparation.

56
00:03:47,266 --> 00:03:50,066
Using antibiotics the right way also helps,

57
00:03:50,066 --> 00:03:53,133
such as taking prescribed courses of antibiotics as directed

58
00:03:53,133 --> 00:03:58,066
to avoid leaving bacteria behind and giving them a chance to become resistant.

59
00:03:58,066 --> 00:04:01,333
Missed doses of antibiotics may allow a better environment

60
00:04:01,333 --> 00:04:06,000
for resistant bacteria to multiply and cause a resistant infection.

61
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:10,233
By matching specific antibiotics to the bacteria that cause the infections,

62
00:04:10,233 --> 00:04:13,200
healthcare providers can fight antimicrobial resistance

63
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:17,666
by reducing the number and strength of antibiotics patients take.

64
00:04:17,666 --> 00:04:23,166
Care should be taken that the infections are not already resistant to the antibiotics!

65
00:04:23,166 --> 00:04:28,833
Also, antibiotics should not be given for viral infections like colds or the flu,

66
00:04:28,833 --> 00:04:32,800
because viruses are not affected by antibiotics.

67
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:36,133
Research at NIAID

68
00:04:36,133 --> 00:04:42,300
NIAID is researching ways to fight the antimicrobial resistance problem.

69
00:04:42,300 --> 00:04:44,300
Many avenues are being examined,

70
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:51,833
including finding new antibiotics that expose weaknesses in the bacterial life cycle,

71
00:04:51,933 --> 00:04:56,666
looking at ways to boost the immune system to fight bacterial infections,

72
00:04:56,666 --> 00:05:02,200
creating bacterial communities that drown out the effects of the infectious bacteria,

73
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:07,666
using special viruses that target and kill infectious bacteria,

74
00:05:07,666 --> 00:05:13,733
and improving diagnostic tests to better target bacteria with the most appropriate antibiotics.

75
00:05:13,733 --> 00:05:17,600
With good public health practices and cutting-edge research,

76
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:23,033
we may be able to keep up with the resistance, and infectious diseases in general,

77
00:05:23,033 --> 00:05:26,733
but we all need to work together to stay one step ahead.

78
00:05:26,740 --> 00:05:31,600
Find out specific up-to-date research and stories from medlineplus.gov

79
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:35,200
or search NIH MedlinePlus magazine,

80
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:42,720
and learn more about NIAID research at niaid.nih.gov.

81
